LAB 7 — WebStrike | CyberDefenders | Network Forensics | Easy

Scenario: An e-commerce web server (shoporoma.com) was compromised through a file upload vulnerability, 
granting the attacker remote code execution via a reverse shell.

Attack Chain

Attacker (117.11.88.124) performs recon via HTTP GET on shoporoma.com → 
uploads malicious file image.jpg.php via /reviews/upload.php (double-extension bypass, Content-Type: application/x-php) → 
embedded PHP payload spawns reverse shell (mkfifo + netcat) to 117.11.88.124:8080 — MITRE ATT&CK T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application) → 
T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer)

Network Indicators

Attacker IP: 117.11.88.124
Compromised web server: shoporoma.com (Apache/2.4.52, Ubuntu)
Uploaded web shell: image.jpg.php
Reverse shell C2 port: 8080

Techniques Identified

Unrestricted file upload — double-extension bypass (image.jpg.php) defeating naive extension-based filtering
Reverse shell — classic mkfifo/netcat one-liner for outbound interactive shell
Tools Used: Wireshark, display filters (http.request.method, tcp.stream)
Concepts Learned: HTTP upload vulnerability analysis, multipart form-data inspection, reverse shell payload deconstruction

